#: manual/design/cmd.md:1
msgid "cmd design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:3
msgid "The `cmd` package is the outermost layer of QED: a file-first command-line tool over the `prover`. This page explains why it is deliberately thin, how it maps prover results to text and exit codes, and what it must never do."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:5
#: manual/design/elab.md:5
#: manual/design/kernel.md:5
#: manual/design/logic.md:5
#: manual/design/parser.md:5
#: manual/design/prover.md:5
#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:5
#: manual/design/tactics.md:5
msgid "Design goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:7
msgid "Check a file of theorem scripts with one command and report every theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:8
msgid "Print diagnostics that a person can act on and a script can parse: a fixed first line per theorem and labelled context lines."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:9
msgid "Exit with a status that build systems can use, with a way to tolerate unfinished proofs during development."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:10
msgid "Add no knowledge of logic, terms or tactics beyond what the prover reports."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:12
#: manual/design/elab.md:9
#: manual/design/kernel.md:17
#: manual/design/logic.md:9
#: manual/design/parser.md:12
#: manual/design/prover.md:12
#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:9
#: manual/design/tactics.md:11
msgid "Mathematical background"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:14
msgid "The tool computes a function from a file to a list of outcomes and a status. Writing $o_1, \\dots, o_n$ for the outcomes of the theorems of a file, each in $\\{\\mathsf{ok}, \\mathsf{err}, \\mathsf{warn}\\}$, the exit status is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:25
msgid "The status is monotone: adding a failing theorem to a file can only raise it, and `--no-warn` only ever lowers a status caused by warnings. A usage error, an unreadable file or a file that does not parse gives 1 without any per-theorem outcomes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:27
#: manual/design/elab.md:33
#: manual/design/kernel.md:104
#: manual/design/logic.md:41
#: manual/design/parser.md:66
#: manual/design/prover.md:34
#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:52
#: manual/design/tactics.md:51
msgid "Design decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:29
msgid "File first"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:31
msgid "**Problem.** A theorem prover's CLI can be a REPL, a single-goal checker or a file checker."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:33
msgid "**Choice.** `qed-cmd` takes exactly one file. Theorems are separated by `qed` lines and checked independently, starting from an empty kernel state with the prelude installed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:35
msgid "**Why.** Files are what users edit, version and pass to CI. Checking each theorem from the same initial state keeps results independent of order, matching the prover's rule that theorems in a file cannot cite each other."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:37
msgid "Thin by rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:39
msgid "**Problem.** A CLI tends to accumulate logic: special cases, its own parsing of goals, its own idea of success."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:41
msgid "**Choice.** The tool only parses arguments, reads the file, calls `prove_theorem_file_results_detailed`, converts the results to strings and computes the exit status. [Code governance](../code_governance.md) makes this a rule: `cmd` is the thinnest layer and consumes only stable prover facades."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:43
msgid "**Why.** Every claim the tool prints comes from the prover, so the prover's tests and the corpus cover it, and the tool cannot become a second proof engine with different semantics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:45
msgid "Stable text, structured first"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:47
msgid "**Problem.** Output must serve both people and tools."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:49
msgid "**Choice.** Each theorem starts with one line `ok …`, `error[kind] …` or `warning[unfinished] …`. Context follows on labelled lines (`step:`, `branch:`, `goal:`, `locals:`, `hole:`, `message:`), with fixed markers `<none>` and `<root>` for absent values. The results are built as structured values (`CmdRunResult`) and rendered in one function."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:51
msgid "**Why.** A fixed first line is easy to grep; labelled lines are easy to read and parse; building values before rendering lets the tests check the structure and the text separately. The goal and locals are rendered with the kernel's structural printer, so the text shows exactly the term the kernel saw, at the cost of verbosity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:53
msgid "Warnings are not successes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:55
msgid "An unfinished proof is a warning, not an `ok`. By default it makes the run fail, so CI does not accept a file with holes. `--no-warn` exists for work in progress: it changes the exit status only, never the output, so the holes stay visible."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:57
#: manual/design/elab.md:61
#: manual/design/kernel.md:234
#: manual/design/logic.md:139
#: manual/design/parser.md:104
#: manual/design/prover.md:72
#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:68
#: manual/design/tactics.md:77
msgid "Correctness and invariants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:59
msgid "**No authority.** The package constructs no theorems and calls no kernel rules; `ok` is printed only for a `ProverItemProved` item, which carries a kernel theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:60
msgid "**Completeness of the report.** Every theorem of a parseable file produces exactly one block, in file order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:61
msgid "**Exit status** follows the formula above; `cmd_exit_code` is its implementation and is covered by `cmd_wbtest.mbt`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:62
msgid "**Positions.** Step numbers and branch paths are the prover's, so they agree with the source and with library use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:64
#: manual/design/elab.md:68
#: manual/design/kernel.md:278
#: manual/design/logic.md:148
#: manual/design/parser.md:112
#: manual/design/prover.md:80
#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:74
#: manual/design/tactics.md:85
msgid "Alternatives rejected"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:66
msgid "**Stopping at the first error.** Faster, but users would have to fix and rerun once per error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:67
msgid "**Hiding warnings with `--no-warn`.** A flag that removes output would let holes go unnoticed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:68
msgid "**Pretty-printing terms in surface syntax.** A printer that re-introduces `∧` and `->` would be a second interpretation of the encodings that could drift from the parser; the structural printer has no such risk."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:70
#: manual/design/elab.md:74
#: manual/design/kernel.md:286
#: manual/design/logic.md:154
#: manual/design/parser.md:119
#: manual/design/prover.md:87
#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:79
#: manual/design/tactics.md:92
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:72
msgid "One file per run; no directories, no imports between files, no watch mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:73
msgid "No REPL and no editor protocol."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:74
msgid "No options beyond `-d` and `--no-warn`; the prelude is always installed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/cmd.md:75
msgid "No library use: the package is an executable and cannot be imported. Library users call the [prover](prover.md) directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:1
msgid "elab design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:3
msgid "The `elab` package fixes the meaning of every name in a term once, at elaboration time, and keeps that meaning when the signature later changes. This page explains the problem it solves, the two judgements it implements, and the stability property that makes it safe to resolve before the kernel checks anything."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:7
msgid "Users write names; the kernel works with constant identities. Between the two sits a scoped signature in which `push`, `add` and `pop` change what a name refers to. The goal is a resolution step whose result does not change meaning behind the user's back: a term resolved in one scope either keeps exactly the constants it was resolved to, or fails, but never silently picks up a newer declaration with the same name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:11
msgid "The [formal specification](../../attachments/qed_formal_spec.typ) separates two judgements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:13
msgid "**Named elaboration** resolves a named term $t$ in a local context $\\Gamma$ and a signature $\\Sigma$ into a resolved term $d$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:19
msgid "Its rules are: a name bound in $\\Gamma$ becomes a variable; otherwise a name visible in $\\Sigma$ becomes the constant $c^{\\iota}_{\\tau \\preceq \\sigma}$ with identity $\\iota$, declared schema $\\sigma$ and occurrence type $\\tau$; applications and abstractions are resolved componentwise, with the binder added to $\\Gamma$. The order \"local, then constant\" is fixed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:21
msgid "**Core typing** checks a resolved term without looking names up:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:31
msgid "and the abstraction rule extends $\\Gamma$. The constant rule reads $\\Sigma(\\iota)$, the declaration with identity $\\iota$, not the declaration currently visible under the name $c$. In the implementation this is the check `id == rc.const_id && schema == rc.schema_ty` in `elab_core_type_of`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:35
msgid "Resolve once, freeze the identity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:37
msgid "**Problem.** If a term stored names and looked them up whenever it was used, the same term could mean two different things before and after a scope change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:39
msgid "**Options.** Re-resolve on every use; store kernel terms only; store resolved terms with identities."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:41
msgid "**Choice.** `RTerm` stores each constant as a `ResolvedConst` with its identity, schema and instance type. Core typing compares these with the state and fails on any difference."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:43
msgid "**Why.** The specification's \"Resolution Freeze under Scope Mutation\" theorem states the property this buys: if $\\Sigma; \\Gamma \\vdash t \\Downarrow d$ and a sequence of push, add and pop operations turns $\\Sigma$ into $\\Sigma'$, then $d$ is unchanged, and every judgement about $d$ either still holds or fails visibly. The proof is that $d$ contains identities, not deferred lookups, and identities are never reused (the kernel allocates them from a monotone counter). Storing only kernel terms would also work for the kernel, but the frontend needs the schema to report instantiation errors and to check terms before lowering."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:45
msgid "Locals before constants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:47
msgid "A local always shadows a constant of the same name. This is the usual lexical scoping, it is the rule the specification fixes, and it is the rule the tactic layer follows for hypothesis names versus theorem names. The examples in the [elab API](../api/elab.md) show a local `c` shadowing a constant `c`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:49
msgid "A separate package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:51
msgid "**Problem.** Resolution could live in the parser, which is its main client."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:53
msgid "**Choice.** It is its own package between the kernel and the parser, depending only on the kernel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:55
msgid "**Why.** The resolution contract is part of the specification and is tested on its own; the parser can change its grammar without touching it, and other frontends can reuse it. The layering `kernel → logic/elab → parser` in [code governance](../code_governance.md) records this."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:57
msgid "Errors as data, typing as an option"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:59
msgid "Resolution returns `Result[_, ElabError]`; core typing returns `HolType?`. A failed lookup has a reason worth reporting (`UnknownName`, `InvalidConstInstance`), while a failed type check is a single fact (`CoreTypingFailure`) whose details the kernel would repeat anyway."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:63
msgid "**Soundness is not at stake.** `elab` builds terms, never theorems. A wrong resolution can only produce a term the kernel rejects or a theorem about a different statement than intended; the second is what freezing prevents."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:64
msgid "**Lowering preserves identities.** `elab_lower_to_term` maps $c^{\\iota}_{\\tau \\preceq \\sigma}$ to the kernel constant $c{:}\\tau$ with identity $\\iota$, so the kernel's admissibility check sees the identity the user resolved, and rejects a theorem whose constants have since been shadowed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:65
msgid "**Round trip.** For a term $t$ built in state $\\Sigma$, `elab_roundtrip_term(Σ', Γ, t)` succeeds exactly when re-resolving $t$ in $\\Sigma'$ gives back the same identities, so it detects scope drift."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:66
msgid "**Equality is built in.** `=` resolves to identity `-1` with schema $\\alpha \\to \\alpha \\to \\mathit{bool}$ in every state; it can be neither declared nor shadowed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:70
msgid "**Lookup at kernel time.** Passing names to the kernel and letting it resolve would put scoping into the trusted base and make theorem meaning depend on the state at use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:71
msgid "**Global unique names.** Requiring every constant name to be unique would remove shadowing and the need for identities, but the specification's scoped signature exists so that local developments can reuse names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:72
msgid "**Type inference.** The resolver checks types that are written down; it does not infer types of binders or instantiate polymorphic constants from context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:76
msgid "No parsing: the [parser](parser.md) turns text into syntax and calls this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:77
msgid "No type inference or unification; every binder type is explicit and constants are used at their schema type unless an instance is requested."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:78
msgid "No overloading, coercions, implicit arguments or type classes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elab.md:79
msgid "No theorems and no authority: lowering produces kernel terms only."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:1
msgid "kernel design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:3
msgid "The kernel is the trusted computing base of QED: the only code that must be correct for a theorem to be believed. This page explains the logic it implements, why its interface makes every other package untrusted, and how the soundness argument of the [formal specification](../../attachments/qed_formal_spec.typ) maps onto the code in `src/kernel`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:7
msgid "A theorem prover is only as trustworthy as the code that can create theorems. QED follows the LCF approach of Milner's Edinburgh LCF and its descendants HOL Light and HOL4:[^lcf] theorems are values of an abstract type whose only constructors are the inference rules of the logic. Parsers, tactics, proof search and the command-line tool may contain any number of bugs, but a bug there can only make a proof fail, never make a false statement a theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:9
msgid "R. Milner, \"LCF: A way of doing proofs with a machine\", 1979; J. Harrison, \"HOL Light: An overview\", TPHOLs 2009. QED follows HOL Light's choice of primitive rules most closely."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:11
msgid "The kernel therefore has three goals:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:13
msgid "implement higher-order logic (HOL) with a small, fixed set of rules that can be read and checked by hand;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:14
msgid "make the theorem type impossible to forge from outside the package;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:15
msgid "grow the theory only through extensions that are provably conservative, and record each one for audit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:21
msgid "Types are generated by type variables and type constructors with fixed arities:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:27
msgid "The constructors `bool` (arity 0), `fun` (arity 2, written $\\sigma \\to \\tau$) and `ind` (arity 0) are built in. A type substitution $\\theta$ maps type variables to types and acts homomorphically. A type $\\tau$ is an *instance* of a schema $\\sigma$, written $\\tau \\preceq \\sigma$, when $\\tau = \\sigma\\theta$ for some $\\theta$; `ty_is_instance_of` decides this by first-order matching."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:31
msgid "Terms are those of the simply typed λ-calculus over a signature of constants:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:37
msgid "A variable is a pair of a name and a type. A constant occurrence $c{:}\\tau$ is legal when $c$ is declared with schema $\\sigma$ and $\\tau \\preceq \\sigma$. The typing judgement is the usual one:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:49
msgid "Typing is syntax-directed and every well-typed term has exactly one type, so `type_of` is a total function into `HolType?`: it makes one pass over the term and, at each application, compares the argument type with the domain of the function type, so its cost is the size of the term times the size of the largest type compared."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:51
msgid "The only logical constants of the core are equality and choice:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:59
msgid "Every other connective is a *definition* in terms of these two, made by the `logic` package through the `DefOK` gate; the [logic design](logic.md) gives the definitions. Keeping connectives out of the kernel keeps the kernel small: it knows nothing about $\\wedge$ or $\\to$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:61
msgid "α-equivalence and De Bruijn terms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:63
msgid "Two terms are α-equivalent when they differ only in the names of bound variables: $\\lambda x.\\,x \\equiv_\\alpha \\lambda y.\\,y$. The rules of HOL must not depend on bound names, so the kernel works on a nameless representation. A De Bruijn term replaces each bound occurrence by the number of binders between it and its binder:[^debruijn]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:71
msgid "N. G. de Bruijn, \"Lambda calculus notation with nameless dummies\", Indagationes Mathematicae 34, 1972."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:73
msgid ""
"The conversion $\\lceil\\cdot\\rceil$ (`to_db_term`) satisfies $t_1 \\equiv_\\alpha t_2 \\iff \\lceil t_1 \\rceil = \\lceil t_2 \\rceil$, so α-equivalence becomes structural equality (`db_term_eq`). Constants carry one more field than in HOL Light, their identity stamp (see \"Every rule is checked against a state\" below), and `db_term_eq` compares it too: an occurrence built with `mk_const` (stamp $-1$, unresolved) and one built with `ks_mk_const` (the resolved identity) are different De Bruijn terms. `db_term_logical_eq` ignores the stamp. QED's De Bruijn terms are *typed*: bound occurrences and binders keep their types. Two consequences follow. Abstractions over different types never collapse, since $\\lceil \\lambda (x{:}\\sigma).\\,x \\rceil \\n"
"e \\lceil \\lambda (x{:}\\tau).\\,x \\rceil$ for $\\sigma \\n"
"e \\tau$. And a term such as $\\lambda (x{:}A).\\,(x{:}B)$, whose inner variable has the name of the binder but another type, has no conversion at all: `to_db_term` returns `None` and every rule reports `BoundaryFailure`. HOL Light reads the inner `x` as a separate free variable; QED rejects the term, so that a binder name always refers to one variable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:75
msgid "Substitution and β-reduction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:77
msgid "Write $\\uparrow^d_c\\,t$ for the shift that adds $d$ to every index $\\ge c$ in $t$, and $t[j \\mapsto s]$ for the replacement of index $j$ by $s$, shifting $s$ as it passes under binders:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:88
msgid "Shifts and replacement act homomorphically on applications and leave free variables and constants alone. β-contraction of $(\\lambda.\\,t)\\,u$ is then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:94
msgid "Why this cannot capture: inside $t$, index $0$ refers to the binder being removed and indices $\\ge 1$ to binders outside it. Shifting $u$ up by one before inserting it makes every free index of $u$ skip the binder that is about to disappear, and the replacement shifts again under each inner binder, so an index of $u$ always counts the binders that actually surround it. After replacement no occurrence of index $0$ remains, because each one was replaced, so the final shift down by one is defined and turns the indices that pointed past the removed binder back into their original values. The named counterpart is the capture-avoiding substitution $t[u/x]$; the specification states this correspondence as the lemma \"Well-Scoped Beta Contraction Safety\". The kernel computes every index with overflow checks and reports `CapacityExceeded` instead of wrapping."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:96
msgid "Substitution for *free* variables (`db_subst_free_parallel`, used by INST) is simpler: a free variable is a name, never an index, and the inserted term is shifted by the current binder depth, which is a no-op for a term with no loose indices. Capture is impossible by construction, which is why INST needs no renaming step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:98
msgid "Sequents and theorems"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:100
msgid "A theorem is a sequent $\\Gamma \\vdash p$: a finite set $\\Gamma$ of propositions (terms of type `bool`) and a proposition $p$. The kernel stores $\\Gamma$ and $p$ as De Bruijn terms and deduplicates hypotheses with `db_term_eq`, so $\\Gamma$ is a set of α-equivalence classes: inserting a hypothesis that is α-equivalent to one already present does nothing (`db_hyps_union`). Because `db_term_eq` also compares constant stamps, two hypotheses that differ only in the stamp of a constant stay two entries, and DEDUCT_ANTISYM_RULE removes only the entry whose stamps match. This can leave an extra hypothesis (the rule is weaker than intended), never a missing one, so it does not affect soundness; build all terms of one proof with `ks_mk_const` to avoid it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:102
msgid "The intended meaning is the standard semantics of HOL: types denote non-empty sets, $\\mathit{bool}$ denotes $\\{\\top, \\bot\\}$, $\\sigma \\to \\tau$ denotes the full set of functions, $=$ denotes identity and $@$ denotes a choice function. A sequent is *valid* when every model and every valuation of the free variables that make all of $\\Gamma$ true also make $p$ true."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:106
msgid "The theorem type is abstract"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:108
msgid "**Problem.** If code outside the kernel could construct a `Thm`, any bug or shortcut in that code could produce a false theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:110
msgid "**Options.** An LCF-style abstract type; proof terms checked by a separate checker (the approach of Coq and Lean); or a trusted record with a \"verified\" flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:112
msgid "**Choice.** `Thm` is declared `type Thm` in the interface: its fields are private and it has no public constructor. The only functions that return a `Thm` are the eleven rule functions (`refl_checked` to `inst_checked`, plus `add_assum_checked`), the gates `ks_define_const_thm` and `ks_specify_const`, the stored-theorem readers `ks_definition_theorem`, `ks_typedef_contract` and `ks_ind_infinity_axiom`, and `thm_bind_const_ids`, which returns its argument with its constant identities resolved again in the given state and checked (see \"Every rule is checked against a state\" below). MoonBit enforces this at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:114
msgid "**Why.** With an abstract type the trusted base is exactly this package. Proof terms would add a second trusted component, the checker, and a large proof object per theorem; QED's specification makes the LCF discipline normative (obligation \"Interface safety\") and checks it by inspecting the interface file."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:116
msgid "HOL Light's primitive rules"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:118
msgid "**Problem.** Choose the rules that generate all theorems."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:120
msgid "**Choice.** The ten rules of HOL Light, the smallest standard basis for HOL with equality as the only primitive connective:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:146
msgid "Matching of premises (the middle term of TRANS, the antecedent of EQ_MP) is up to α-equivalence and ignores constant identities (`db_term_logical_eq`), because both theorems have already been checked against the current state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:148
msgid "One deviation: QED's BETA accepts any redex $(\\lambda x.\\,t)\\,u$, whereas HOL Light's primitive BETA only accepts $(\\lambda x.\\,t)\\,x$ and derives the general form with INST. The general form is a derived rule in HOL Light, so this adds no theorems; it saves the kernel a renaming step and is exactly the rule stated in the specification."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:150
msgid "**Why HOL and not dependent types.** HOL has a simple, well-understood set-theoretic semantics, a kernel of a few hundred lines in HOL Light, and decades of experience showing that the ten rules suffice for mathematics when combined with definitions. The kernel stays small enough for its soundness argument to be read in full, which is the point of a kernel-first design."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:152
msgid "Weakening is provided natively"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:154
msgid "`add_assum_checked` implements weakening: for a proposition $q$, $\\Gamma \\vdash p$ gives $\\Gamma \\cup \\{q\\} \\vdash p$. It is not one of the ten rules and the specification does not list it, but it is a derived rule, so it adds no theorems. Both $p$ and $q$ have type $\\mathit{bool}$ (every conclusion does, and the rule checks $q$), so the two rules below apply:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:165
msgid "The hypothesis set of line 4 is $\\Gamma \\cup \\{q\\}$: the part $\\{q\\} \\setminus \\{p\\}$ is contained in $\\{q\\}$, and $(\\Gamma \\setminus \\{q\\}) \\cup \\{q\\} = \\Gamma \\cup \\{q\\}$. The derivation works whether or not $q \\equiv_\\alpha p$ or $q \\in \\Gamma$, and it only uses set operations on hypotheses, so it gives the same hypothesis set as the native rule whatever equality the kernel uses to compare hypotheses. Every premise and result is checked against the same state, as in the native rule. The native rule is a shortcut used by replay in the `logic` package to make hypothesis sets match exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:167
msgid "A named boundary over a De Bruijn core"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:169
msgid "**Problem.** Users and the frontend think in named terms; the rules must be name-independent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:171
msgid "**Options.** Named terms with explicit renaming (HOL Light); locally nameless terms; De Bruijn terms everywhere."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:173
msgid "**Choice.** The interface takes and returns named `Term` values; every rule converts its inputs with `to_db_term`, works on `DbTerm`, and converts back with `from_db_term` only when a caller asks for hypotheses or a conclusion. A failed conversion is the error `BoundaryFailure`, not a derivation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:175
msgid "**Why.** α-equivalence becomes equality, substitution needs no fresh names, and hypothesis sets are sets of α-classes for free. The cost is that terms read back from a theorem carry generated binder names (`_b0`, `_b1`, ...), so callers compare with `term_alpha_eq`. The specification proves the commutation square that justifies the boundary: lowering, running the De Bruijn rule and lifting gives a result α-equivalent to running the named rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:177
msgid "Every rule is checked against a state"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:179
msgid "**Problem.** Constants are declared in scopes and can be shadowed. A theorem about the constant `c` proved in one scope must not be reused after an inner scope declares a different `c`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:181
msgid "**Choice.** Every rule takes the `KernelState` and runs `ensure_thm_admissible` on its premises and on its result. A theorem records the identity (`ConstId`) of each constant it mentions. It is admissible in a state when each recorded identity is the one the state resolves the name to, each constant occurrence is an instance of the declared schema, each type uses only admitted constructors, and a definition theorem still agrees with its definition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:183
msgid "**Why.** Name lookup changes as scopes are pushed and popped, but a recorded identity does not. Freezing identities makes resolution stable under scope mutation (the specification's \"Resolution Freeze\" theorem), and the check turns a stale theorem into an `InvalidInstantiation` failure instead of a silent change of meaning. The tutorial shows a theorem that is rejected inside a shadowing scope and accepted again after the scope is popped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:185
msgid "**One exception: `thm_bind_const_ids`.** An occurrence built with `mk_const` has stamp $-1$, and the identity the theorem records for it is taken from the state when the theorem is built. `thm_bind_const_ids(state, th)` does not check that record: it resolves every unstamped occurrence again in `state` and records the new identity. Applied after a scope has shadowed a constant `c`, it turns a theorem about the outer `c` into a theorem about the inner one. This is sound, because only constants without a definition can be shadowed: `ks_add_const` and both definition gates refuse names that are already definition, representation or abstraction heads, so a shadowed constant is uninterpreted, and a theorem about an uninterpreted constant stays valid when another constant of the same type takes its place, just as INST may replace a free variable. It does break the letter of the freeze property, so code that relies on frozen identities should build constants with `ks_mk_const`, whose occurrences carry their identity and cannot be rebound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:187
msgid "Extensions go through gates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:189
msgid "The theory grows in three ways, each guarded by a gate that checks side conditions and appends an `ExtensionCert`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:191
msgid "**`DefOK`, constant definition.** `ks_define_const(c, \\tau, t)` adds a constant $c : \\tau$ and the theorem $\\vdash c = t$. Each side condition rules out a known way to break conservativity:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:193
msgid "Condition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:193
msgid "Error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:193
msgid "Counterexample it prevents"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:195
msgid "$t$ is closed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:195
msgid "$c = x$ would give $\\vdash c = x$, then INST gives $\\vdash c = y$ and so $\\vdash x = y$ for all $x, y$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:196
msgid "No constant named $c$ occurs in $t$, directly or through earlier definitions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:196
msgid ""
"$c = \\n"
"eg c$ would give $\\vdash c = \\n"
"eg c$, a contradiction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:197
msgid "$c = (\\forall x{:}\\alpha.\\,\\forall y{:}\\alpha.\\,x = y)$ with $c : \\mathit{bool}$ is true when $\\alpha$ has one element and false at $\\alpha = \\mathit{bool}$; INST_TYPE gives both instances of the definition, with the same left-hand side $c$, so $\\vdash \\top = \\bot$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:198
msgid "$c$ has never been defined"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:198
msgid "Two definitions of one name would give $\\vdash c = t_1$ and $\\vdash c = t_2$, so $\\vdash t_1 = t_2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:200
msgid "The counterexamples in the second and last rows are stated, as in the specification, for constants identified by name. In the code the new constant always receives a fresh identity, so a name $c$ that occurs in $t$ can only denote an older constant, and a second definition of $c$ in an inner scope would get a new identity too; neither could then produce the contradiction shown. The two checks are kept anyway, by name and over the whole theory history: they make every definition read as non-circular and unique when constants are read by name, which is what the specification's freshness conditions require, and they keep the unfolding argument below simple. They are deliberately stricter than identities need: `ks_define_const` refuses `c := d` in an inner scope when an earlier definition of `d` mentions an outer, undefined constant named `c`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:202
msgid "Under these conditions a definition is conservative. Every instance of the new constant is $c{:}\\tau\\theta$ for some type substitution $\\theta$, and replacing it by $t\\theta$ maps each proof in the extended theory to a proof in the old theory: the definition theorem $\\vdash c = t$ and its type instances become instances of REFL, every other rule step maps to the same step, and a theorem that does not mention $c$ is mapped to itself. The ghost-variable condition is what makes the replacement a function of the instance $c{:}\\tau\\theta$ alone. The specification proves this as \"Definition-level conservativity\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:204
msgid "**`TypeDefOK`, type definition.** `ks_register_type_definition` admits a type $\\kappa(\\bar\\alpha)$ in bijection with $\\{x : \\rho \\mid P\\,x\\}$, given a hypothesis-free theorem $\\vdash P\\,w$ for a closed term $w$. It declares the representation $\\mathit{rep} : \\kappa(\\bar\\alpha) \\to \\rho$, named by the caller, and the abstraction $\\mathit{abs} : \\rho \\to \\kappa(\\bar\\alpha)$, named `Abs_` followed by $\\kappa$, and returns three contract theorems, with $a : \\kappa(\\bar\\alpha)$ and $r : \\rho$ free variables:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:214
msgid "The first says that $\\mathit{rep}$ is injective, the second that its image lies inside $P$, and the third that every element of $P$ is in the image. HOL Light characterises a type bijection by the first theorem and the equivalence $P\\,r = (\\mathit{rep}(\\mathit{abs}\\,r) = r)$. The third theorem is the left-to-right direction of that equivalence, and the right-to-left direction follows from the second theorem instantiated at $a := \\mathit{abs}\\,r$ and rewritten with the hypothesis $\\mathit{rep}(\\mathit{abs}\\,r) = r$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:216
msgid "The witness matters because the second contract theorem holds for a free variable $a$. If $P$ were empty, for example $P = \\lambda x.\\,\\bot$, it would read $\\vdash \\bot$: HOL types denote non-empty sets, so no model can interpret a type in bijection with an empty set, and the logic, in which every type is inhabited, proves the contradiction. The gate also requires the predicate's type variables to be among the parameters $\\bar\\alpha$, for the same reason that `DefOK` forbids ghost type variables."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:218
msgid "**`SpecOK`, constant specification.** `ks_specify_const` introduces a constant $c : \\tau$ with the property $\\vdash P\\,c$, given a hypothesis-free theorem $\\vdash P\\,w$ for a closed term $w$. The specification asks for $\\vdash \\exists x.\\,P\\,x$ instead; a concrete witness is a stronger premise, since it implies the existential. The gate defines $c := @P$ through `DefOK`, which records a `DefOK` certificate, then builds $\\vdash P\\,c$ itself and records a `SpecOK` certificate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:220
msgid "The theorem $\\vdash P\\,c$ is not derived by kernel rules. It is an instance of the choice axiom"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:226
msgid "at $x = w$, rewritten with the definition $c = @P$, and the kernel has no rule or theorem for the choice axiom: the specification takes the axiom schema as part of its trusted baseline. The theorem is valid because $@$ denotes a choice function in every model: $\\vdash P\\,w$ makes $[\\![P]\\!]$ non-empty, so $[\\![@P]\\!] \\in [\\![P]\\!]$, and $c$ denotes $[\\![@P]\\!]$. For the same reason the extension is conservative over the old theory *together with the choice axiom*: in a derivation, each use of $\\vdash P\\,c$ can be replaced by the choice instance at $w$ and the definition, and then $c$ unfolded as for `DefOK`. The conservativity of `DefOK` alone does not cover this step, because $\\vdash P\\,c$ does not follow from $c = @P$ without the axiom. The specification proves the combined statement as \"Conservativity of Specification Extension\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:228
msgid "**Infinity anchor.** HOL needs an infinite type for arithmetic, and the specification assumes that `ind` is one. `ks_register_ind_infinity_axiom` stores a theorem as the anchor of that assumption, but it accepts any hypothesis-free admissible theorem whose statement mentions `ind`, even $\\vdash x = x$ for $x : \\mathit{ind}$, and it does not check that the theorem says `ind` is infinite. Since the kernel has no axiom of infinity, no theorem it can produce says so. The anchor therefore adds no theorem and no risk to consistency; it only marks, for audit, the model-class restriction of the specification. Arithmetic over `ind` is not available."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:230
msgid "Results, not exceptions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:232
msgid "Every kernel function returns `Result` or `Option`; none aborts on bad input. A rule that cannot apply says why, with a constructor of `LogicError` or `SigError`, and the caller decides what to do. This is what makes the frontend fail closed: the `tactics` and `prover` packages turn these values into structured diagnostics, and no path turns a failed rule into a theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:236
msgid "Why soundness reduces to the kernel"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:238
msgid "Call a theorem value *sound* when its sequent is valid in every model of the current theory. The argument has three steps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:240
msgid "**1. Every rule preserves validity.** For each primitive rule, valid premises give a valid conclusion. Two cases show the pattern."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:242
msgid ""
"ABS. Let $M$ be a model and $v$ a valuation that satisfies $\\Gamma$. Since $x \\n"
"otin \\mathrm{FV}(\\Gamma)$, every valuation $v[x \\mapsto a]$ also satisfies $\\Gamma$, so by validity of the premise $[\\![s]\\!]_{v[x \\mapsto a]} = [\\![t]\\!]_{v[x \\mapsto a]}$ for every $a$. Hence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:248
msgid "by function extensionality in the standard model. Without the side condition the step \"every $v[x \\mapsto a]$ satisfies $\\Gamma$\" fails: from $\\{x = 0\\} \\vdash x = 0$ one could derive $\\{x = 0\\} \\vdash (\\lambda x.\\,x) = (\\lambda x.\\,0)$, which is false whenever $x = 0$ holds and the type of $x$ has a second element."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:250
msgid "DEDUCT_ANTISYM_RULE. Let $v$ satisfy $(\\Gamma \\setminus \\{q\\}) \\cup (\\Delta \\setminus \\{p\\})$. If $[\\![p]\\!]_v = \\top$, then $v$ satisfies $\\Delta$ (the only hypothesis that may have been removed from $\\Delta$ is $p$), so $[\\![q]\\!]_v = \\top$. Symmetrically $[\\![q]\\!]_v = \\top$ implies $[\\![p]\\!]_v = \\top$. Two booleans that imply each other are equal, so $[\\![p = q]\\!]_v = \\top$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:252
msgid "The remaining rules follow the same way: REFL and TRANS from reflexivity and transitivity of identity, MK_COMB from congruence of application, BETA from the substitution lemma $[\\![t[u/x]]\\!]_v = [\\![t]\\!]_{v[x \\mapsto [\\![u]\\!]_v]}$, EQ_MP from the meaning of $=$ on booleans, ASSUME trivially, INST from the substitution lemma for free variables (a valid sequent holds under every valuation, in particular under $v[x \\mapsto [\\![u]\\!]_v]$), and INST_TYPE because a valid sequent holds under every interpretation of its type variables by non-empty sets, and every type the state admits denotes one. The specification proves each case (\"Rule-level preservation\")."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:254
msgid "**2. Every extension preserves consistency.** Every model of the old theory in which $@$ is a choice function extends to such a model of the new one: interpret a defined constant by its right-hand side, a new type by the non-empty subset its witness exhibits, with $\\mathit{rep}$ the inclusion and $\\mathit{abs}$ any retraction onto it, and a specified constant by the chosen element $[\\![@P]\\!]$. The theorems the gates return are valid in the extended model, as argued above, so no false sentence in the old language becomes provable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:256
msgid "**3. Interface safety.** Because `Thm` is abstract, every `Thm` that exists at run time is the root of a finite derivation tree whose nodes are rule applications and gate outputs. `add_assum_checked` is a derived rule, as shown above; the readers `ks_definition_theorem`, `ks_typedef_contract` and `ks_ind_infinity_axiom` return gate outputs stored in the state; and `thm_bind_const_ids` changes only constant identities, in the way shown to be sound above. Induction on the depth of the tree, using steps 1 and 2, shows that every `Thm` is sound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:258
msgid "Step 3 is a property of the code, not of the logic, and it is why nothing outside `src/kernel` needs to be trusted: the `logic`, `tactics` and `prover` packages can only call the functions in the [kernel API](../api/kernel.md), so whatever they compute, any `Thm` they return has a derivation. The specification states the six obligations and their dependencies; the conformance pack in `formal_verification/` checks the paper side in Lean."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:260
msgid "Invariants maintained by the code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:262
msgid "A `Thm` stores its hypotheses as a list without duplicates under `db_term_eq` (α-equivalence with equal constant stamps) and its conclusion as a De Bruijn term; every rule result passes `ensure_thm_admissible` in the state it was built in."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:263
msgid "`KernelState` is persistent. A gate returns a new state; the base state stays valid, so `ks_conservative_replay_ok(base, extended, th)` can re-check `th` against both."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:264
msgid "Constant identities are allocated from a counter in the theory state and never reused, even after a scope is popped. (The `sig_*` functions on a bare `GlobalSig` number new constants after the largest identity still visible, so they can reuse one after a pop; they never touch theorems.)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:265
msgid "Definition heads, type-definition heads and the infinity anchor are recorded in the theory history, which `ks_pop_scope` does not touch: a name, once defined, cannot be defined again."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:267
msgid "Complexity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:269
msgid "Write $n$ for the size of the terms involved, $d$ for their binder depth, $h$ for the number of hypotheses, $k$ for the number of constant occurrences in a theorem and $D$ for the number of declarations in the signature. The costs are polynomial in these sizes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:271
msgid "Lowering a named input with `to_db_term` searches the binder environment at each variable and copies it at each abstraction, $O(n \\cdot d)$. Reading a term back with `from_db_term`, as `thm_concl` and `thm_hyps` do, also searches the names already in use, $O(n \\cdot (d + f))$ with $f$ the number of free names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:272
msgid "The rule cores walk their terms once. BETA and INST shift the inserted term at every occurrence they replace, so their cost is the size of the result, at most $O(n \\cdot m)$ for an inserted term of size $m$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:273
msgid "Hypothesis union and removal compare hypotheses pairwise, $O(h_1 h_2 n)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:274
msgid "`ensure_thm_admissible` runs on every premise and on every result. It collects constant names with linear membership tests, $O(k^2)$, and looks each occurrence up in the scoped signature and matches it against the schema, $O(k \\cdot D)$ plus the size of the types."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:276
msgid "Proof sizes in the shipped subset are small; the kernel favours checks that are easy to audit over asymptotic speed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:280
msgid "**Named terms with renaming, as in HOL Light.** Every rule would need a correct renaming function, which is a classic source of kernel bugs. The De Bruijn core avoids renaming entirely."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:281
msgid "**Connectives as kernel primitives.** Adding $\\wedge$, $\\to$ or $\\forall$ as primitive constants with their own rules would enlarge the kernel and its soundness proof. They are definitions over $=$ instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:282
msgid "**Exceptions for rule failure.** HOL Light raises `Failure`. Results make every failure visible in the type and keep the frontend from accidentally catching and ignoring one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:283
msgid "**Unchecked rules with a separate validation pass.** Checking admissibility only at the end would let an inadmissible intermediate theorem feed later steps. Every rule checks its inputs and output instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:284
msgid "**Arbitrary axioms.** There is no function that turns a term into a theorem. The only non-derived theorems are definitions, type-definition contracts and the property of a specified constant, all produced by gates with side conditions that keep the extension conservative."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:288
msgid "The kernel does not parse text, elaborate names or run tactics; those are the [elab](elab.md), [parser](parser.md) and [tactics](tactics.md) packages, and none of them is trusted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:289
msgid "It implements no connective other than equality and choice, and no quantifier syntax; the [logic](logic.md) package defines the rest."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:290
msgid "It does not store proved theorems by name. Theorem names are a frontend concern."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:291
msgid "It has no metavariables and no incomplete theorems: a `hole` in a proof script never reaches the kernel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:292
msgid "It does not prove its own soundness. The argument above and in the specification is a paper proof; `formal_verification/` aligns the specification with Lean, not the MoonBit source."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/kernel.md:293
msgid "It does not provide the choice axiom as a theorem. `@` is declared and used by `SpecOK`, but no public function returns $P\\,x \\to P(@P)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:1
msgid "logic design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:3
msgid "The `logic` package turns the equality calculus of the kernel into propositional logic. It defines the connectives as kernel definitions, derives the natural-deduction rules from the ten primitive rules, and keeps the catalog of theorem names that proof scripts may cite. This page gives the definitions, derives the rules, and explains why the package can do all this without being trusted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:7
msgid ""
"The kernel knows only equality and choice. Users want $\\top$, $\\bot$, $\\wedge$, $\\Rightarrow$, $\\n"
"eg$ and $\\vee$ with their usual rules. The goal is to provide them with no new authority: every connective is a definition admitted by the `DefOK` gate, and every rule is a MoonBit function that calls kernel rules. The package may be wrong in the sense of failing to prove something, but it cannot prove anything false."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:11
msgid "Connectives as definitions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:13
msgid "QED follows the definitions of HOL Light, where every connective is reduced to equality.[^hol-defs] Let $t$ be the truth term $(\\lambda x.\\,x) = (\\lambda x.\\,x)$. The prelude defines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:26
msgid "J. Harrison, *HOL Light Tutorial*, section on the logical constants; the definitions go back to Andrews' type theory Q0. QED's disjunction differs from HOL Light's, see below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:28
msgid "where a primed name stands for the definition body already expanded, so that each right-hand side is a closed term over $=$ alone. The reading of each definition:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:30
msgid "$t$ is true because it is an instance of REFL."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:31
msgid "$\\bot$ says the identity predicate on booleans equals the constantly-true predicate, that is $\\forall p.\\,p$ with $\\forall P := (P = \\lambda x.\\,t)$. It is false in the standard model because the two predicates differ at $p = \\text{false}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:32
msgid "$p \\wedge q$ says the pair $(p, q)$ cannot be told apart from $(t, t)$ by any function $f$, which holds exactly when $p$ and $q$ are both true."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:33
msgid "$p \\Rightarrow q$ is $(p \\wedge q) = p$: adding $q$ to $p$ changes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:34
msgid ""
"$\\n"
"eg p$ is $p \\Rightarrow \\bot$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:35
msgid ""
"$p \\vee q$ is $\\n"
"eg p \\Rightarrow q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:37
msgid "Basis terms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:39
msgid "`prop_mk_and` and its siblings return the *basis* form, the expanded right-hand side, rather than the application $\\mathit{and}\\,p\\,q$ of the constant. The rules below work on basis forms, because only on those can the kernel rules act directly; the constants exist so that the definitions are recorded, can be cited, and can be recognised in terms that users write."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:43
msgid "Derive, do not postulate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:45
msgid "Every rule of the package is a derivation. The derivations below are the ones the code performs; each line is a kernel rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:47
msgid "**Truth.** From the definition $\\vdash \\top = t$, symmetry gives $\\vdash t = \\top$, and $\\vdash t$ is REFL (`logic_prop_truth_thm`), so EQ_MP gives $\\vdash \\top$ (`logic_prop_truth_const_thm`). Symmetry (`logic_eq_sym`) is itself derived:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:58
msgid "**From a proof to an equation with truth.** From $\\Gamma \\vdash p$ and $\\vdash \\top$, DEDUCT_ANTISYM_RULE gives $(\\Gamma \\setminus \\{\\top\\}) \\cup (\\emptyset \\setminus \\{p\\}) = \\Gamma \\setminus \\{\\top\\} \\vdash p = \\top$, and $\\Gamma \\setminus \\{\\top\\} = \\Gamma$ unless $\\top$ is itself a hypothesis, in which case dropping it is harmless because $\\top$ is provable. This \"EQT_INTRO\" step is how propositions are put inside terms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:60
msgid "**Conjunction introduction.** From $\\Gamma \\vdash p$ and $\\Delta \\vdash q$, EQT_INTRO gives $\\Gamma \\vdash p = \\top$ and $\\Delta \\vdash q = \\top$. Congruence with the constant $\\mathit{and}$ then gives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:70
msgid "and symmetry with EQ_MP turns a proof of $\\vdash \\mathit{and}\\,\\top\\,\\top$ into $\\Gamma \\cup \\Delta \\vdash \\mathit{and}\\,p\\,q$. That proof needs no hypotheses: the definition of $\\mathit{and}$, applied to $\\top, \\top$ and β-normalised, gives $\\vdash \\mathit{and}\\,\\top\\,\\top = \\big((\\lambda f.\\,f\\,\\top\\,\\top) = (\\lambda f.\\,f\\,t\\,t)\\big)$, and the right-hand side is proved for a variable $f$ by"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:81
msgid "ABS applies because the hypothesis set is empty, and the abstracted terms are closed apart from $f$, so nothing is captured. Finally the definition of $\\mathit{and}$ is unfolded in $\\Gamma \\cup \\Delta \\vdash \\mathit{and}\\,p\\,q$ and the result β-normalised, which gives the basis form $(\\lambda f.\\,f\\,p\\,q) = (\\lambda f.\\,f\\,t\\,t)$, that is $p \\wedge q$. This route uses ABS only on closed terms. The textbook route, MK_COMB on $\\Gamma \\vdash p = t$ and $\\Delta \\vdash q = t$ followed by ABS on $f$, would need $f$ to be fresh for $p$, $q$ and $\\Gamma \\cup \\Delta$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:83
msgid "**Conjunction elimination.** Apply both sides of $\\Gamma \\vdash (\\lambda f.\\,f\\,p\\,q) = (\\lambda f.\\,f\\,t\\,t)$ to the selector $\\lambda x\\,y.\\,x$ with MK_COMB and REFL, then reduce both sides with BETA and TRANS:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:91
msgid "Symmetry and EQ_MP with $\\vdash t$ give $\\Gamma \\vdash p$. The selector $\\lambda x\\,y.\\,y$ gives $q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:93
msgid "**Implication elimination.** From $\\Gamma \\vdash (p \\wedge q) = p$ and $\\Delta \\vdash p$: symmetry gives $\\Gamma \\vdash p = (p \\wedge q)$, EQ_MP gives $\\Gamma \\cup \\Delta \\vdash p \\wedge q$, and conjunction elimination gives $q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:95
msgid "**Implication introduction.** From $\\Gamma \\vdash q$: conjunction introduction with $\\{p\\} \\vdash p$ gives $\\Gamma \\cup \\{p\\} \\vdash p \\wedge q$, and elimination from the assumption gives $\\{p \\wedge q\\} \\vdash p$. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:101
msgid "and the hypothesis set is $\\Gamma \\setminus \\{p\\}$. The conclusion is $p \\Rightarrow q$ by definition. The derivation works whether or not $p \\in \\Gamma$: when $p$ is absent, the conjunction step adds it and DEDUCT_ANTISYM_RULE removes it again, giving the vacuous implication $\\Gamma \\vdash p \\Rightarrow q$. `logic_prop_imp_intro_thm` nevertheless refuses a $p$ that is not a hypothesis of the premise. This is a check on the caller, not a limit of the logic; for a vacuous implication, weaken with `logic_add_assum` first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:103
msgid "**Ex falso.** From $\\Gamma \\vdash (\\lambda p.\\,p) = (\\lambda p.\\,t)$ and any proposition $q$, MK_COMB with $\\vdash q = q$ and two BETA steps give $\\Gamma \\vdash q = t$, hence $\\Gamma \\vdash q$. Negation elimination is implication elimination with conclusion $\\bot$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:105
msgid ""
"**Disjunction introduction.** From $\\Gamma \\vdash p$: assume $\\n"
"eg p$, eliminate it against $p$ to get $\\Gamma \\cup \\{\\n"
"eg p\\} \\vdash \\bot$, derive $q$ by ex falso, and discharge $\\n"
"eg p$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:111
msgid ""
"The hypotheses are $\\Gamma$ unless $\\n"
"eg p$ was already among them. From $\\Delta \\vdash q$ the right introduction must discharge a $\\n"
"eg p$ that the proof of $q$ does not use. Because `logic_prop_imp_intro_thm` only discharges a hypothesis that is present, it first conjoins $\\{\\n"
"eg p\\} \\vdash \\n"
"eg p$ with $\\Delta \\vdash q$ and eliminates the conjunction again, which gives $\\Delta \\cup \\{\\n"
"eg p\\} \\vdash q$, and then discharges $\\n"
"eg p$: $\\Delta \\setminus \\{\\n"
"eg p\\} \\vdash p \\vee q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:113
msgid "No disjunction elimination"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:115
msgid ""
"The prelude defines $p \\vee q$ as $\\n"
"eg p \\Rightarrow q$. Introduction is derivable, as shown. Elimination, from $p \\vee q$, $p \\Rightarrow r$ and $q \\Rightarrow r$ infer $r$, is not: it needs a case split on $p$, that is excluded middle $p \\vee \\n"
"eg p$. In HOL excluded middle follows from the choice axiom and extensionality (Diaconescu's theorem),[^diaconescu] but the kernel exposes no theorem for the choice axiom and the package does not derive excluded middle. Rather than ship a rule it cannot justify, the catalog has `or_intro_l` and `or_intro_r` and no `or_elim`, and the tactic layer has `left` and `right` but no case analysis."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:117
msgid "R. Diaconescu, \"Axiom of choice and complementation\", Proc. AMS 51, 1975. HOL Light derives `EXCLUDED_MIDDLE` this way in `class.ml`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:119
msgid "Trusted recognition of connective constants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:121
msgid "**Problem.** A user can declare a constant named `and` that means something else. If `prop_dest_and` recognised any application of a constant called `and`, a tactic could treat an arbitrary term as a conjunction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:123
msgid "**Choice.** The destructors accept the basis form, which is checked structurally, and an application of a connective constant only when the state holds the canonical definition theorem for that constant (with the current identity). `install_prop_prelude` refuses to install over a placeholder constant with the right name and type but no definition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:125
msgid "**Why.** Recognition errors would not break soundness, because every rule is replayed through the kernel. They would produce confusing failures at replay time instead of clear failures at the tactic, and the specification requires connector recognition to be backed by definitions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:127
msgid "One catalog, two modes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:129
msgid "**Problem.** `exact th` and `apply th` mean different things. `exact` needs a theorem whose conclusion is the goal; `apply` needs an implication whose consequent is the goal and leaves its antecedent as the new goal. Letting a name be used in the wrong mode silently would either fail late or, worse, make `exact` quietly behave like `apply`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:131
msgid "**Choice.** Each catalog entry records an `exact_class` and an `apply_class`. `exact` consults only the first, `apply` only the second, and a name that exists but is unusable in a mode resolves to `KnownButUnavailable`, which the tactics layer reports as a shape or apply mismatch. A local hypothesis name always takes precedence over a catalog name and never falls back to it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:133
msgid "**Why.** The table is the single source for the tactics layer, the prover's corpus, the mapping matrix and the documentation, so the theorem names a user may write cannot drift between them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:135
msgid "Errors from the kernel only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:137
msgid "Kernel error constructors are read-only outside the kernel. The package obtains the `LogicError` and `SigError` values it returns by running small kernel operations that are known to fail in the required way. This keeps the error vocabulary owned by the kernel, at the cost of less specific errors: many helper failures surface as `TypeMismatch` or `AlphaMismatch`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:141
msgid "**Soundness.** Every `Thm` returned by this package is the result of kernel functions, so the kernel's soundness argument covers it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:142
msgid "**Coverage, not completeness.** The derivations above show that each rule *can* succeed whenever its premises have the stated shapes. The code can still fail on such premises in corner cases: β-normalisation is bounded (see below), and the connective builders choose fixed binder names, so `prop_mk_and` captures a free variable of its arguments that is literally named `_p_and` and has type $\\mathit{bool} \\to \\mathit{bool} \\to \\mathit{bool}$; the resulting term is not $p \\wedge q$, and `logic_prop_and_intro_thm` then fails. Such failures are reported as errors, never as theorems."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:143
msgid "**Conservativity of the prelude.** The six constants are admitted by `DefOK` with closed right-hand sides, no type variables and no cycles, so the prelude is a conservative extension of the empty theory."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:144
msgid "**Idempotence.** On a state that already holds the six canonical definitions, `install_prop_prelude` returns its argument unchanged, so installing twice is the same as installing once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:145
msgid "**Exact sequents.** The replay helpers check hypotheses as sets up to α-equivalence. `logic_prop_strengthen_to_hyps` only adds hypotheses, so a theorem that needs a hypothesis the goal does not have is rejected rather than accepted with extra assumptions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:146
msgid "**β-normalisation is proof-producing.** `logic_beta_normalize_eq` and `logic_normalize_prop_beta` build kernel equations step by step; the term-level `logic_beta_nf_*` functions return the right-hand side of such an equation. `logic_beta_normalize_eq` contracts only head redexes, with no step bound; this terminates because simply typed terms are strongly normalising. `logic_normalize_prop_beta` and `logic_beta_nf_bool_term` contract leftmost-outermost redexes along application spines, never under an abstraction, and stop after 512 steps even if redexes remain; the deep form repeats such passes up to 32 times. These bounds suffice for the connective encodings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:150
msgid "**Connectives as kernel primitives.** This would add rules to the kernel and to its soundness proof. Definitions cost nothing in trust."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:151
msgid "**HOL Light's disjunction** $\\forall r.\\,(p \\Rightarrow r) \\Rightarrow (q \\Rightarrow r) \\Rightarrow r$. With it, elimination is derivable without excluded middle, at the cost of a universal quantifier over propositions inside every disjunction. The prelude uses the shorter encoding and states its limit instead; switching would change every disjunction term and the corpus built on them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:152
msgid "**A mixed resolver.** `logic_prop_resolve_ref` resolves names without distinguishing modes; it is kept for tests, while the tactics layer uses the mode-specific resolvers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:156
msgid "The package proves propositional facts only; it has no quantifier rules beyond what the tactics layer builds for theorem-header binders."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:157
msgid "It has no disjunction elimination, no excluded middle and no classical reasoning."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:158
msgid "It does not store user theorems: the catalog is fixed in the source, and adding a name means adding code and a test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:159
msgid "It adds no authority. Any change to it is reviewed for usefulness, not for soundness."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/logic.md:160
msgid "It does not parse or print formulas; terms are built by the [parser](parser.md) and printed by the kernel's structural printer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:1
msgid "parser design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:3
msgid "The `parser` package turns text into syntax trees and syntax trees into kernel terms. It is untrusted and deliberately narrow: it fixes the surface notation, reports errors at positions the user can find, and hands goals upward as plain data. This page explains the grammar, the lowering path and the boundary to the tactics layer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:7
msgid "Accept a small, unambiguous notation for propositional goals and proof scripts, with Unicode as the canonical form and ASCII spellings as input conveniences."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:8
msgid "Report every syntax error at an offset in the text the user wrote, even after normalisation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:9
msgid "Lower to kernel terms only through the resolution boundary of `elab` and the connective builders of `logic`, so the parser never decides what a name or a connective means."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:10
msgid "Stay below the tactics layer: produce goals, never proof states."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:14
msgid "Grammar"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:16
msgid "Terms follow this grammar, where *name* is an identifier and application is juxtaposition:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:27
msgid "Negation is a prefix operator that binds tighter than every infix operator, so `¬ a ∧ b` is `(¬ a) ∧ b`. The ambiguity of the first production is resolved by precedence and associativity:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:29
msgid "Reading of a chain"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:31
msgid "`a = b = c` is an error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:32
msgid "`a ∧ b ∧ c` is `(a ∧ b) ∧ c`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:33
msgid "`a ∨ b ∨ c` is `(a ∨ b) ∨ c`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:34
msgid "`a -> b -> c` is `a -> (b -> c)`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:36
msgid "Goals are sequents $h_1, \\dots, h_n \\vdash c$, and a goal may start with `forall (x : A), ...`. Theorem scripts add a header with binders and a body of steps, with optional `{ ... }` branch blocks after `split`, `left` and `right`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:38
msgid "Operator-precedence parsing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:40
msgid "An infix chain $t_0\\ o_1\\ t_1\\ \\dots\\ o_n\\ t_n$ is parsed with an operator stack. When a new operator $o$ arrives with an operator $o'$ on top of the stack, the parser reduces $o'$ first exactly when"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:48
msgid "keeps $o'$ when the precedence is lower or both are right-associative, and fails with `NonAssocChain` when the precedences are equal and one of them is non-associative. Each operator is pushed and popped once, so the algorithm is linear in the length of the chain, and it produces the unique tree that respects the table."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:52
msgid "Lowering maps syntax to kernel terms:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:64
msgid "Names go through `elab` (locals before constants, identities frozen), connectives through the `logic` builders, which check that their arguments are propositions. A goal `forall (x : A), body` is lowered by adding $x{:}A$ to $\\Gamma$ and lowering `body`: the quantifier is dropped and $x$ stays free in the goal, exactly as for a theorem-header binder `(x : A)`. A theorem with a free variable holds for every value of it, because INST can substitute any term of the same type, so $\\vdash \\mathit{body}$ with $x$ free is the HOL reading of the quantified statement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:68
msgid "Normalise first, keep an offset map"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:70
msgid "**Problem.** Users type `\\and`, `|-` or extra spaces; error messages must point at what they typed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:72
msgid "**Choice.** `normalize_parser_input` rewrites the input once into canonical text and records, for every character, its raw offset. The lexer and parser work on the canonical text, and every error offset and span is mapped back before it leaves the package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:74
msgid "**Why.** One canonical form keeps the grammar small, and the map keeps diagnostics honest. The old ASCII spellings `/\\` and `\\/` are no longer accepted, so there is one ASCII spelling per connective."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:76
msgid "Raw parsing separate from lowering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:78
msgid "**Problem.** Lowering needs a kernel state; tools such as the prover need the script structure before any state exists, for example to report the position of a step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:80
msgid "**Choice.** Every construct has a `_raw` parser that returns a syntax tree with positions and no state, and a lowering function that takes the state. Theorem scripts are only parsed raw; the prover lowers their goals itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:82
msgid "**Why.** The prover can blame a failing step by its span and index without re-parsing, and the parser stays independent of proof execution."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:84
msgid "A parser-owned goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:86
msgid "**Problem.** The obvious return type for `parse_goal` is the tactics package's `Goal`, but then the parser would depend on the tactics layer and changes there would ripple into syntax."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:88
msgid "**Choice.** The parser returns its own `ParsedGoal`; the prover converts it with `@tactics.mk_goal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:90
msgid "**Why.** It keeps the layer order `kernel → logic/elab → parser → tactics → prover` acyclic, as [code governance](../code_governance.md) requires, and it means the parser cannot construct proof objects even by mistake."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:92
msgid "Connectives are built, not looked up"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:94
msgid "Connectives are lowered with the `logic` builders to basis terms; the parser does not require constants named `and` or `imp` to exist. This gives every connective the same meaning in every state, and lets the parser report a non-proposition argument as `Logic(NotBoolTerm)` at lowering time instead of leaving it to a tactic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:96
msgid "Quantifiers only as goal sugar"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:98
msgid "Raw `forall` is accepted at the start of a goal and nowhere else. A general binder in terms would need a quantifier constant and its rules in the logic layer, which the shipped subset does not have. Accepting it only at the start of a goal, where it means the same as a theorem-header binder, keeps the surface honest: anything that lowers can be proved by the existing machinery. `forall` inside a term is refused with `UnexpectedToken`. In a term the raw parser already refuses it, at its position. In a goal the raw parser accepts it after an operator, as in `⊢ p -> forall (x : bool), x`, and the error comes from lowering; that error carries offset 0 instead of the position of `forall`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:100
msgid "Positions on every step"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:102
msgid "Every script step records its index, its branch path and its span. These are the fields the prover and the CLI report on failure and for unfinished proofs, so a user sees `step: 3`, `branch: 1` and the source text instead of a bare error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:106
msgid "**No authority.** The parser builds terms and, through `parse_def_function`, calls the kernel's `DefOK` gate. It produces no theorem by any other route."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:107
msgid "**Offsets refer to raw input.** For every `ParseError` and `SourceSpan`, offsets are positions in the original string, within $[0, \\text{raw length}]$. Lowering errors carry no position of their own: `Elab`, `Logic` and `Sig` errors have none, and the few `Parse` errors raised during lowering, such as the nested `forall` above, use offset 0."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:108
msgid "**Deterministic structure.** The precedence table defines one tree for every accepted chain; chains of `=` are rejected rather than guessed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:109
msgid "**Locals before constants.** A binder or `let` local shadows a constant with the same name, consistently with `elab` and the tactics layer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:110
msgid "**Step numbering.** `step_index` counts steps from 1 in source order across the whole script, including steps inside branch blocks, so the number in a diagnostic matches the reading order of the script."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:114
msgid "**A parser generator.** The grammar is small enough that a hand-written lexer and operator-precedence parser is shorter, gives better error offsets, and needs no build step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:115
msgid "**Returning tactics objects.** Rejected for layering, as above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:116
msgid "**Full HOL term syntax** with typed binders, type annotations and quantifiers everywhere. It would invite scripts the tactics layer cannot prove; the syntax grows only together with the proof machinery."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:117
msgid "**Implicit `T` and `F`.** They are ordinary constant names resolved through the state, so a script fails visibly when the prelude is missing instead of silently using another meaning."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:121
msgid "No type inference: binder and `let` types are written explicitly; there are no type annotations inside terms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:122
msgid "No `forall` or `exists` inside terms, no λ-abstraction syntax, and no user-defined operators."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:123
msgid "No pretty-printer: terms are rendered by the kernel's structural printers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/parser.md:124
msgid "No proof execution: steps are parsed into `SynTacticStep` values, run by the [tactics](tactics.md) package and scheduled by the [prover](prover.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:1
msgid "prover design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:3
msgid "The `prover` package turns a theorem script into one of three outcomes: a kernel theorem, a structured failure, or an unfinished proof. This page explains that result model, how branch blocks are scheduled on top of the tactics layer, and why the package fails closed: no path through it can report success without a kernel theorem for the stated goal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:7
msgid "Run theorem scripts end to end: parse, install the prelude, lower the goal, execute steps, schedule branch blocks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:8
msgid "Tell the user exactly where a proof stops: theorem, step number, branch path, source text, current goal and local hypotheses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:9
msgid "Never present anything but a kernel `Thm` as a proof. Unsupported input, failing steps and holes are reported as such."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:10
msgid "Keep the documentation honest by publishing the corpus of scripts that the tests run and the manual quotes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:14
msgid "Outcomes as a sum type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:16
msgid "For a script with goal $\\Gamma \\vdash c$ the result is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:22
msgid "The three cases are disjoint, and only the first carries a theorem. In particular an unfinished proof is not a theorem with an extra assumption: it is a report containing the goal $\\Gamma' \\vdash c'$ that the hole stands for. Turning the hole into a hypothesis would give the theorem $\\Gamma \\cup \\{c'\\} \\vdash c$, which is a different statement from the one the user asked for."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:24
msgid "Branch blocks as nested proofs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:26
msgid "A step followed by branch blocks, such as `split { s₁ } { s₂ }`, runs the step on the current goal $G$, producing subgoals $G_1, G_2$. Each block $s_i$ is then a separate proof of $G_i$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:32
msgid "The prover runs $s_i$ in a fresh proof state rooted at $G_i$ (`ps_isolate_pending_at`), obtains a theorem $t_i$ for $G_i$ from that state, and closes $G_i$ in the parent state with $t_i$. Because the parent's justification for `split` is valid, the theorems compose into a theorem for $G$; this is the LCF composition of justifications described in the [tactics design](tactics.md), applied one level up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:36
msgid "Three-way results instead of `Result`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:38
msgid "**Problem.** A `Result[Thm, Error]` would force an unfinished proof to be either a theorem, which is false, or an error, which loses the difference between \"wrong\" and \"not done yet\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:40
msgid "**Choice.** `ProverRunResult` has three constructors. `prove_theorem_script` still returns a `Result` for callers that only want a theorem, but its error side `ProverScriptError` keeps failures and unfinished proofs apart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:42
msgid "**Why.** Editors and the command-line tool treat the cases differently: an error stops the user, an unfinished proof is a warning with a goal to work on. The specification requires that a hole never produce theorem authority; the type makes that impossible to get wrong."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:44
msgid "Diagnostics carry positions and context"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:46
msgid "Every failure and unfinished report records the theorem name, the step index, the branch path, the step's span and source text, and the goal and locals at that point, all in terms of the raw input. The `cmd` package prints these fields directly. The cost is a wide record; the benefit is that no consumer has to re-run anything to explain a result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:48
msgid "Isolated frames for branch blocks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:50
msgid "**Problem.** Running branch bodies inside the parent state would let a failure in one branch disturb the bookkeeping of its sibling, and would make the reported branch path depend on scheduling details."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:52
msgid "**Choice.** Each branch body runs in its own proof state built from one pending goal, without the parent's replay context. When the body finishes, `ps_close_frame` must return a theorem for that goal; the parent closes the goal with it. A body that leaves goals open is a failure at the branch step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:54
msgid "**Why.** Each block is then an independent proof of its subgoal, which is what the block syntax suggests, and step indices and branch paths are assigned in reading order regardless of nesting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:56
msgid "Files continue after a bad theorem"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:58
msgid "**Problem.** A file with several theorems should report all problems, not only the first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:60
msgid "**Choice.** `prove_theorem_file_results_detailed` runs every theorem and returns one item each. Only an unparseable file or a failure to install the prelude fails the whole file. Proved theorems are not added to the state: later theorems cannot cite earlier ones by name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:62
msgid "**Why.** Reporting every theorem is what a user of the CLI expects. Not adding theorems to the state keeps the catalog of citable names fixed, as the [user manual](../manual.md) states; a theorem environment is not part of the shipped subset."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:64
msgid "The prelude is installed by option"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:66
msgid "With `auto_install_prelude` on, which is the default, the prover installs the propositional prelude before running. Scripts can then use `T`, `F` and catalog theorems without preparing a state. Tests that need a specific state turn it off and install constants themselves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:68
msgid "The corpus is code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:70
msgid "The scripts that the manual and these pages quote are values in `corpus.mbt`: positive, negative, unfinished and quantifier cases, each with an identifier and the capability or failure it shows, and a mapping matrix from case to documentation anchor. The tests run every case and check the mapping, so a published example cannot drift from what the code does. The conformance guide makes this a rule for public examples."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:74
msgid "**Fail closed.** `Proved` is constructed in one place, from the result of `ps_qed`, which returns a theorem only after the tactics layer checked it against the root goal. Every other path returns `Failed` or `Unfinished`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:75
msgid "**No authority.** The package calls the parser, the tactics layer and kernel state functions; it does not build theorems itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:76
msgid "**Holes do not leak.** A hole stops the script before `ps_qed`; there is no code path that turns an unfinished state into a theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:77
msgid "**Positions are raw.** Offsets and spans in reports refer to the input string as given, through the parser's offset map."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:78
msgid "**Ordering.** Items of a file report are in source order, and step indices count steps in reading order across branch blocks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:82
msgid "**Exceptions for failures.** Results keep every outcome visible in the type and let the CLI render all of them uniformly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:83
msgid "**Holes as assumptions.** Rejected because the resulting theorem would state something other than the goal, as shown above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:84
msgid "**A shared state for branch bodies.** Simpler to implement, but it couples sibling branches and makes blame depend on scheduling."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:85
msgid "**Theorem environment across a file.** Letting later theorems cite earlier ones needs a naming and replay discipline that the specification does not yet define; until then the catalog is the only source of names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:89
msgid "The prover does not add steps; the step set is that of [tactics](tactics.md) plus `hole`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:90
msgid "It does not read files or print; the [cmd](cmd.md) package does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:91
msgid "It does not search for proofs, rewrite or simplify. The research prototype in [research_rewrite](research_rewrite.md) is not wired in."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/prover.md:92
msgid "It does not store proved theorems or let scripts refer to each other."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:1
msgid "research_rewrite design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:3
msgid "The `research_rewrite` package explores how QED could offer rewriting and simplification without giving up the kernel-first discipline. It is research-only: it is not used by the shipped packages, and the review recorded in `research/rewrite-simplify/` decided not to promote it on the audited baseline. This page describes the model it implements and the constraints any promotion would have to meet."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:7
msgid "Rewriting replaces a subterm by an equal one. In an LCF system every such replacement must be justified by an equation theorem, so a rewriter is really a producer of theorems $\\vdash t = t'$. The prototype tests whether that can be done for QED's goals with a small, auditable set of witnesses, and whether the steps can be recorded so that a later stage can check them again."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:11
msgid "Conversions and congruence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:13
msgid "A *conversion* maps a term $t$ to a theorem $\\Gamma \\vdash t = t'$. Rewriting at a position inside a larger term needs congruence: if $\\Gamma \\vdash l = r$, then for every context $C[\\cdot]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:19
msgid "For a context made only of applications this follows by induction on the path to the hole, one MK_COMB per step:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:27
msgid "with REFL supplying the unchanged side. This is what a path of `CombFun` and `CombArg` site steps produces, recorded as one `Congruence` step per site step. Under an abstraction the step would be ABS, which requires that the bound variable not occur free in $\\Gamma$; with a local hypothesis as witness that side condition can fail, and the prototype does not support `AbsBody` sites."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:29
msgid "Witnesses"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:31
msgid "The equation $l = r$ at the focus comes from one of four witnesses:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:33
msgid "Witness"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:33
msgid "Theorem"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:33
msgid "Recorded steps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:35
msgid "$\\{l = r\\} \\vdash l = r$ by ASSUME"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:36
msgid "the given theorem"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:37
msgid "the connective's definition applied to its arguments and β-normalised"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:38
msgid "REFL followed by BETA and TRANS steps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:40
msgid "Right-to-left rewriting with an equation adds `Symmetry`. A rewrite with a local equality carries that equation as a hypothesis, so it is only valid in a goal where the equation is a hypothesis; replaying it without the local fails, as the tutorial shows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:42
msgid "Using a rewrite in a proof"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:44
msgid "To turn a rewritten goal back into a proof of the original, one needs $\\Gamma \\vdash C[l] = C[r]$ and a proof of $C[r]$; EQ_MP with the symmetric equation then proves $C[l]$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:50
msgid "The prototype builds the equation internally but does not expose it, and no tactic performs this last step. That is the main reason it is not part of the shipped path."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:54
msgid "Record obligations, rebuild to check"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:56
msgid "**Choice.** A rewrite returns the new conclusion and a `ReplayObligation`: the request, the before and after conclusions, and the kinds of kernel steps used. `research_validate_replay_obligation` checks an obligation by rebuilding every segment from its request with kernel rules and comparing the result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:58
msgid "**Why.** Obligations are plain data that can be stored, compared and reviewed, as the promotion gates of the research line require. Because checking rebuilds instead of trusting the record, a forged or stale obligation is rejected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:60
msgid "Honest failure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:62
msgid "Every unsupported case returns `HonestFailure` with a reason: non-boolean conclusions, `AbsBody` sites, folding a definition, β-expansion, unknown locals, witnesses that do not match the focus, and exceeding the step limit. Nothing degrades into a silent no-op except the explicit `NoChange`, which means that no rule applied."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:64
msgid "Bounded simplification"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:66
msgid "`research_simplify_term_concl` alternates unfolding, β-normalisation and the user's requests until a fixed point, and stops with a failure when the number of segments would exceed `step_limit`. The bound makes termination a property of the configuration rather than of the rule set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:70
msgid "**No authority.** Every equation the package builds comes from kernel and `logic` functions, and no theorem leaves the package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:71
msgid "**Checkable records.** An obligation validates exactly when every segment can be rebuilt from its request against the given state, prelude and locals, and the segments chain from `before_concl` to `after_concl`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:72
msgid "**Termination.** Simplification performs at most `step_limit` segments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:76
msgid "**Returning theorems directly.** It would make the prototype a de facto tactic without the review the research process requires."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:77
msgid "**Rewriting under binders with renaming.** It needs the ABS side condition to be managed and was left out of the evaluated scope."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:81
msgid "Research-only, not shipped, not imported by any other package, and not reachable from theorem scripts or the command-line tool."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:82
msgid "No rewriting under abstractions, no folding of definitions, no β-expansion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:83
msgid "No proof of the original goal: the package returns terms and obligations, not theorems."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/research_rewrite.md:84
msgid "The authoritative status of this line is `research/README.md` and the documents in `research/rewrite-simplify/`, not this page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:1
msgid "tactics design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:3
msgid "The `tactics` package lets a user prove a goal backwards, by reducing it to simpler goals, while every theorem is still built forwards by the kernel. This page explains the LCF view of tactics as theorem transformers, how QED records the forward half of each step as data instead of closures, and why an incorrect tactic can make a proof fail but never make it succeed wrongly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:7
msgid "Offer goal-directed steps (`intro`, `split`, `left`, `right`, `apply`, `exact`, `assumption`) whose meaning matches natural deduction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:8
msgid "Produce a kernel `Thm` for exactly the goal the user stated, or fail with a reason and the location of the failing goal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:9
msgid "Hold no authority: the package only calls `logic` and kernel functions to build theorems."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:13
msgid "Tactics as theorem transformers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:15
msgid "In LCF a goal is a sequent $\\Gamma \\vdash c$ still to be proved, and a tactic is a function"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:21
msgid "that returns subgoals $g_1, \\dots, g_n$ and a *justification* $j$. The tactic is *valid* when, for theorems $t_1, \\dots, t_n$ that prove $g_1, \\dots, g_n$, the theorem $j(t_1, \\dots, t_n)$ proves the original goal.[^gordon] Running a proof means applying tactics until no goals remain, then composing the justifications bottom-up, starting from theorems that close leaves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:23
msgid "M. Gordon, R. Milner, C. Wadsworth, *Edinburgh LCF*, LNCS 78, 1979. A sequent $\\Gamma' \\vdash c'$ *proves* a goal $\\Gamma \\vdash c$ when $c' \\equiv_\\alpha c$ and $\\Gamma' \\subseteq \\Gamma$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:25
msgid "The key property is that validity is a matter of *correctness*, not of *soundness*. A justification can only build theorems by calling kernel rules. If a tactic is invalid, its justification produces a theorem for some other goal, or fails; it cannot produce a false theorem. LCF therefore checks validity at the end, and QED does the same."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:27
msgid "The justifications of QED's steps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:29
msgid "Each step below is a valid tactic; the justification is the kernel derivation in the right column."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:31
msgid "Goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:31
msgid "Subgoals"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:31
msgid "Justification"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:33
msgid "$t \\mapsto$ discharge $a$ from $t$ (implication introduction)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:34
msgid "$(t_1, t_2) \\mapsto$ conjunction introduction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:35
msgid "$t \\mapsto$ disjunction introduction on the left"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:36
msgid "$t \\mapsto$ disjunction introduction on the right"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:37
msgid "$t \\mapsto$ modus ponens with $\\{a \\Rightarrow b\\} \\vdash a \\Rightarrow b$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:38
msgid "ASSUME $c$, weakened to $\\Gamma$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:39
msgid "the catalog theorem for $n$, weakened to $\\Gamma$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:41
msgid "Validity of each line is the corresponding natural-deduction rule, derived in the [logic design](logic.md). For `intro` on an implication the subgoal has $a$ among its hypotheses, so implication introduction can discharge it, and the result has hypotheses $\\Gamma$ again."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:43
msgid "`intro x` also applies to a goal of the form $(\\lambda y.\\,P) = (\\lambda y.\\,\\top)$, with $\\top$ the constant `T`, which is HOL's encoding of $\\forall y.\\,P$. The kernel reads the two abstractions back with a generated binder name $z$ (`_b0`, `_b1`, ...) that is fresh for the conclusion, and the subgoal is $\\Gamma \\vdash P[z/y]$ with $z$ now free; the local `x` names it. When the subgoal is proved, the justification renames $z$ to a variable $x'$ chosen fresh against the goal, its locals and its hypotheses, and abstracts:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:49
msgid "The last line is the goal up to α-equivalence. When $z$ does not occur free in $\\Gamma$, INST leaves $\\Gamma$ unchanged and ABS applies, because $x'$ is fresh by construction. But $z$ is only fresh for the conclusion. If a hypothesis happens to contain a free variable with the generated name, the subgoal $\\Gamma \\vdash P[z/y]$ speaks about that variable, a step may close it using the hypothesis, and the replay then fails with `Logic(VarFreeInHyp)`. The step is then not a valid tactic in the LCF sense, but the failure is caught by the kernel, as described below. Goals from theorem scripts never reach this case, because the parser lowers `forall` by dropping the quantifier, not into this encoding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:53
msgid "Justifications as data"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:55
msgid "**Problem.** LCF represents a justification as a closure. A closure cannot be inspected, so it cannot report where in a proof it failed, and it captures the whole context it was built in."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:57
msgid "**Choice.** A pending goal carries its justification as data: an implication prefix (hypotheses to discharge), a refinement chain of `PendingRefine` values (backward `apply` and quantifier steps to undo), a `SplitRole` (left or right half of a conjunction) and an `OrContext` (which side of a disjunction was chosen). When a goal closes, `finish_goal_evidence` interprets that data: it replays the refinement chain, then wraps disjunctions, merges conjunction halves, discharges the implication prefix, and finally undoes quantifier steps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:59
msgid "**Why.** The data is the defunctionalised form of the closure: each constructor corresponds to one justification in the table above, and the interpreter applies them. It can be inspected for diagnostics, it is immutable, and two proof states can share structure safely."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:61
msgid "Replay at every close, check at the root"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:63
msgid "**Problem.** If the forward replay waited for the very end, an invalid step early in a proof would be reported only at `ps_qed`, far from its cause."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:65
msgid "**Choice.** Evidence is replayed as soon as a goal closes. The first half of a `split` stores its theorem in the second half's `SplitRole`; when the second half closes, both are merged. When the last goal closes, the theorem is compared with the root goal: after β-normalisation, its hypotheses must match the root hypotheses one to one and its conclusion must equal the root conclusion, both up to α-equivalence. Only then is it stored as the final theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:67
msgid "**Why.** Failures appear at the step that causes them, and the final comparison is exactly LCF's validity check: if every step were valid it would always succeed, and if some step is not, the user gets `ProofSynthesisUnavailable` instead of a theorem for the wrong statement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:69
msgid "Two name spaces, one order"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:71
msgid "`exact n` and `apply n` look up `n` first among the locals introduced by `intro`, then among the catalog names of the `logic` package. A local always wins and never falls back to a catalog name, so a local named `truth` cannot be mistaken for the theorem `truth`. The catalog is consulted in the mode of the step: `exact` uses only names that can close a goal outright, and `apply` only names that are implications. A name used in the wrong mode is reported as a mismatch, not as an unknown name."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:73
msgid "No hole step"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:75
msgid "A proof with a gap is not a proof. The tactics layer has no step that leaves a goal open, so it cannot produce a state that pretends to be finished. Holes are a frontend concept: the prover stops at a hole and reports an unfinished proof with the goal at that point."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:79
msgid "**Soundness.** Every theorem is built by `logic` and kernel functions; the tactics layer cannot construct a `Thm`. A bug here makes a proof fail or report the wrong error, never succeed with a false theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:80
msgid "**Root fidelity.** A proof state has a final theorem only if that theorem proves the root goal, up to β-normal form and α-equivalence, with exactly the root hypotheses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:81
msgid "**Goal order.** New subgoals are placed in front of the remaining goals, in order. Together with `Split` storing the left half's theorem in the right half, this gives the left-to-right evaluation that the prover's branch blocks rely on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:82
msgid "**Branch paths.** Each pending goal records the path of choices from the root: `Split` appends 1 or 2, `Left` and `Right` append 1. The prover and the CLI print this path on failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:83
msgid "**Persistence.** `ps_apply` never changes its argument; a failed step leaves the previous state usable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:87
msgid "**Closures for justifications.** Simpler to write, but opaque for diagnostics and harder to test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:88
msgid "**Building theorems only at `ps_qed`.** Fewer kernel calls, but errors would surface far from their cause."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:89
msgid "**Implicit `exact`-to-`apply` fallback.** Letting `exact` silently start a backward step when a name is an implication would make scripts harder to read and errors harder to place. The modes are separate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:90
msgid "**Metavariables.** Leaving parts of a goal to be filled later, as Isabelle or Lean do, would need unification and a notion of incomplete theorem in the kernel. The shipped subset does not need it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:94
msgid "Propositional steps only, plus `intro` on the HOL encoding of a universal quantifier. No rewriting, no case analysis on disjunctions, no induction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:95
msgid "No proof search: every step is given by the user. `assumption` and `apply imp_elim` only search the current hypotheses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:96
msgid "No parsing and no scheduling of branch blocks; the [prover](prover.md) does both."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/tactics.md:97
msgid "No holes and no partial theorems."
msgstr ""

